---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BriefingForm from '../../components/BriefingForm.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/CallToAction.astro';




---

<BaseLayout title="Director Liability & Governance Risk - Decoheron">
    <div class="container py-4">
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="row mb-5">
            <div class="col-lg-9">
                <span class="badge bg-primary mb-3 px-3 py-2 text-uppercase fs-6">
                    Board Governance & Fiduciary Risk
                </span>
                <h1 class="display-4 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">
                    Cyber Risk is Personal Liability
                </h1>
                <p class="lead text-subtle fs-5">
                    Cybersecurity is a non-delegable board duty. Incomplete controls expose directors to personal regulatory penalties, shareholder lawsuits, and voided insurance coverage.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Statutory & Regulatory Frameworks -->
        <div class="mb-5">
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold text-primary">Obligations That Apply to Every Australian Business</h2>
                <p class="text-secondary fw-medium">These duties do not depend on your industry, your regulator, or whether you hold a licence.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="row g-4">
                <!-- Corporations Act s180/181 -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-6">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Corporations Act 2001</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">Sections 180 & 181</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                            Directors must exercise care, diligence, and good faith. Accepting management assurances about cyber posture without independent technical verification is not diligence. Where controls are neither evidenced nor tested, a board has no factual basis for the assurances it has been giving itself.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Privacy Act Amendments -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-6">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Privacy Act 1988</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">Privacy & NDB Scheme</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                           Civil penalties exceeding $50 million apply for serious or repeated interference with privacy. Businesses over $3 million turnover are covered, and health service providers are covered regardless of turnover. Failing to take reasonable steps to secure personal information is itself the breach, separate from whatever the attacker does with it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Cyber Security Act 2024 -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-6">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Cyber Security Act 2024</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">Mandatory Ransomware Payment Reporting</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                           Since 30 May 2025, any business carrying on business in Australia with turnover above $3 million must report a ransomware or cyber extortion payment to government within 72 hours. The obligation captures payments made overseas and payments made by a third party on your behalf. Not-for-profits are not exempt. Late reporting attracts a civil penalty.
                        </p> 
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">  
                           There is no grace period and no discretion. If you pay, you disclose, and that disclosure becomes a record of the incident and the controls that failed to prevent it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                        <!-- Contractual Flow-Down -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-6">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Contractual Flow-Down</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">Customers, Insurers, and Acquirers</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                           The fastest-growing source of security obligation is not a regulator. It is your largest client's supplier security schedule, your insurer's renewal questionnaire, and the diligence checklist in your next transaction.
                           These are contractual commitments. Signing one you cannot evidence is a misrepresentation with commercial consequences, whether or not any regulator ever looks at it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div> 
            </div>   
        </div>    

        <div class="mb-5">
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold text-primary">Obligations That Depend on Your Sector</h2>
                <p class="text-secondary fw-medium">Select the regime your business operates under..</p>
            </div>   
            <div class="row g-4"> 

                <!-- Financial Services -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Financial Services</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">AFS Licensees & APRA-Regulated Entities</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                            AFS licensees must maintain adequate risk management systems and adequate technological and human resources under section 912A of the Corporations Act. APRA-regulated banks, insurers, and superannuation funds carry additional obligations under CPS 234 and CPS 230, with boards held directly accountable for operational resilience and third-party risk.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Legal and Property -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Legal, Accounting & Property</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">AML/CTF Tranche 2</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                            Since 1 July 2026, AUSTRAC obligations extend to lawyers, conveyancers, accountants, real estate professionals, trust and company service providers, and dealers in precious metals and stones. Roughly 80,000 to 100,000 businesses were captured for the first time.
                            These firms must now collect and retain customer identification, beneficial ownership, and source of funds records for seven years. Passports. Driver's licences. Bank details. Most have added the obligation without adding a single control to protect it. Penalties reach $33 million for a body corporate.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Healthcare & Aged Care -->
                <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                    <div class="card bg-light h-100 p-4 rounded-3 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg">
                        <span class="text-secondary fw-bold text-uppercase fs-6 mb-2">Healthcare & Aged Care</span>
                        <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary mb-3">Privacy Act & My Health Records</h3>
                        <p class="mb-0 text-body fs-6">
                            Health service providers are covered by the Privacy Act regardless of turnover, so the small business exemption does not apply. My Health Records adds a further layer of obligation and its own penalty regime. Health has been consistently among the most-notified sectors under the notifiable data breach scheme since it commenced.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

 
            </div>    
        </div>
        <!-- Critical Governance Failure Points -->
        <div class="row justify-content-center mb-5">
            <div class="col-lg-10">
                <div class="p-4 border-0">
                    <h2 class="h3 fw-bold text-primary mb-4 text-center">
                        Structural Exposure in Traditional Defense
                    </h2>

                    <div class="row g-4">
                        <!-- Item 1 -->
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="d-flex align-items-start gap-3">
                                <span class="square-bullet mt-1">■</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary">Insurance Policy Voidance</h3>
                                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                                        Control attestations, such as claiming full MFA coverage without verified, continuously monitored enforcement, create material misrepresentation. In the event of a breach, insurers routinely deny coverage for gaps that were attested but never evidenced.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Item 2 -->
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="d-flex align-items-start gap-3">
                                <span class="square-bullet mt-1">■</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary">Legacy SOC & Alert Noise</h3>
                                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                                        Traditional Security Operations Centers (SOCs) generate high alert volume but provide low structural defense. Reactive monitoring creates an illusion of security, leaving boards legally exposed when alerts are missed or ignored.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Item 3 -->
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="d-flex align-items-start gap-3">
                                <span class="square-bullet mt-1">■</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary">AI Threat Velocity</h3>
                                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                                        AI-driven social engineering, automated credential harvesting, and deepfake authorised push payments bypass legacy perimeter defenses in seconds. Manual review processes can no longer meet the legally required standard of care.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Item 4 -->
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="d-flex align-items-start gap-3">
                                <span class="square-bullet mt-1">■</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold text-primary">Personal & Derivative Liability</h3>
                                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                                        D&O insurance policies increasingly exclude losses resulting from gross cyber negligence. Directors risk personal assets, disqualification, and shareholder derivative suits when security postures fail to meet statutory standards.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>


        <!-- Technical Implications for the Board -->
        <div class="row g-4 mb-5">
            <div class="col-12">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold text-primary mb-1">
                    Technical Implications for the Board
                </h2>
                <p class="text-secondary fw-medium mb-0">Bridging statutory expectations with underlying technical realities</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Essential Eight Level 0 -->
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card bg-light h-100 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg p-4 rounded-3">
                    <div class="mb-3">
                        <h3 class="h4 fw-bold text-primary mb-1">Essential Eight ML0</h3>
                        <p class="text-secondary mb-0 fw-medium">Statutory Negligence & Un-audited Blind Spots</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-body mb-3">
                        Under Section 180 of the Corporations Act, a director relying on the business judgment rule needs to show they informed themselves about the subject matter to the extent they reasonably believed appropriate. Sitting at Essential Eight ML0, undocumented and unverified, is difficult to reconcile with that standard.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Fallacy of Self-Assessed Compliance -->
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card bg-light h-100 border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg p-4 rounded-3">
                    <div class="mb-3">
                        <h3 class="h4 fw-bold text-primary mb-1">Self-Assessment Fallacy</h3>
                        <p class="text-secondary mb-0 fw-medium">Paper Compliance vs. Forensic Reality</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-body mb-3">
                        Standards like CPS 234 and SOCI require technical controls to actively function in practice, not just exist on paper.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                        Static audit reports and self-assessments fail under regulatory examination when post-incident forensic evidence reveals unmitigated vulnerabilities.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Non-Delegable Vendor Risk -->
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card h-100 bg-light border border-2 border-secondary shadow-lg p-4 rounded-3">
                    <div class="mb-3">
                        <h3 class="h4 fw-bold text-primary mb-1">Non-Delegable Vendor Risk</h3>
                        <p class="text-secondary mb-0 fw-medium">Outsourced IT & MSP Accountability</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-body mb-3">
                        Regulators reject the defence that cyber security has been fully delegated to a managed service provider (MSP).
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-body mb-0">
                        When a vendor compromise reaches your environment, the board remains accountable for failing to enforce controls over external access that were verified to work, not merely assumed to.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <CallToAction />
    </div>
    <BriefingForm hidden={true} />
</BaseLayout>
